# Implementing a Figma design

When given a Figma link, read the design through the Figma MCP server first.
Never guess at a layout from a screenshot alone when the file is available.

Then, before writing anything:

1. List the elements in the frame and match each one to an existing component in
   `src/components/ui/`. Reuse beats recreate.
2. Match every colour, radius and font size to a token in `src/theme/tokens.js`.
   If the design uses a value within a few percent of an existing token, use the
   token — designs drift, and a near-match is almost always the same intent.
   Only add a token when the value is genuinely new, and say that you did.
3. Say which parts of the design you cannot build with what exists, and what you
   propose adding, before you build them.

While building:

- Figma's own code output is a starting hint, not the answer. It emits absolute
  positions and fixed pixel sizes; translate those into flexbox with `gap`, and
  into tokens. Never paste it in unchanged.
- A fixed frame width in Figma means "designed at this width", not "hard-code
  this width". Let content fill the available space.
- Export icons and images through the MCP asset endpoint. If it returns a local
  source, use that source directly rather than adding an icon package or
  inventing a placeholder.
- Reproduce spacing as `gap` on the parent, not margins on children.

Afterwards, state clearly where the implementation deviates from the design and
why.
